WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:02.580
- Cosmopolitan UK put this on the cover

00:00:02.580 --> 00:00:04.510
and it stirred a lot of controversy.

00:00:04.510 --> 00:00:08.060
- But let's focus on whether
obese people are healthy

00:00:08.060 --> 00:00:09.230
because they're not.

00:00:09.230 --> 00:00:11.160
- I sat down with Arghavan Salles,

00:00:11.160 --> 00:00:14.140
a renowned bariatric surgeon
and obesity researcher

00:00:14.140 --> 00:00:15.820
to get her thoughts on the cover,

00:00:15.820 --> 00:00:18.380
as well as the popular
body positivity movement

00:00:18.380 --> 00:00:21.140
known as health at every size.

00:00:21.140 --> 00:00:22.560
Do you think overall,

00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:24.320
the health at every eize movement

00:00:24.320 --> 00:00:27.520
is an overall net positive,
net negative or neither?

00:00:27.520 --> 00:00:30.657
- That very line, health at every size

00:00:30.657 --> 00:00:33.040
is not actually true.

00:00:33.040 --> 00:00:34.860
Can there be people at any size

00:00:34.860 --> 00:00:37.570
who are healthy at that moment? Yes.

00:00:37.570 --> 00:00:40.320
But is it true that no
matter what your size

00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:43.880
you're always able to be
healthy, like that's not true.

00:00:43.880 --> 00:00:48.111
I am anti-weight bias, anti fat-phobia,

00:00:48.111 --> 00:00:51.420
anti-fat stigma, definitely
anti-fat shaming,

00:00:51.420 --> 00:00:52.400
I've written about that,

00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:55.630
but I don't think that being aware

00:00:55.630 --> 00:00:57.370
of the consequences of obesity

00:00:57.370 --> 00:00:58.740
is the same as fat shaming.

00:00:58.740 --> 00:01:01.190
- What I'm hearing, and
correct me if I'm wrong,

00:01:01.190 --> 00:01:05.560
is you could be a patient who
is diagnosed with obesity,

00:01:05.560 --> 00:01:07.810
may have great cholesterol,
may be not diabetic,

00:01:07.810 --> 00:01:09.290
their blood pressure may be great.

00:01:09.290 --> 00:01:13.280
The fact that their weight
is above a certain cutoff,

00:01:13.280 --> 00:01:15.440
it puts them at a greater
risk down the line,

00:01:15.440 --> 00:01:16.890
maybe not at this given moment.

00:01:16.890 --> 00:01:18.500
- All those lab values also don't mean

00:01:18.500 --> 00:01:21.300
that they don't have knee pain or hip pain

00:01:21.300 --> 00:01:23.980
or ankle pain or lower back pain,

00:01:23.980 --> 00:01:26.098
which are all things that we commonly see

00:01:26.098 --> 00:01:27.960
with people with obesity.

00:01:27.960 --> 00:01:30.230
Increased risk of endometrial
cancer, for example,

00:01:30.230 --> 00:01:32.040
increased risk of mood disorders,

00:01:32.040 --> 00:01:34.250
like all of that is still there,

00:01:34.250 --> 00:01:36.670
even if our lab values are normal.

00:01:36.670 --> 00:01:39.144
- [Presenter] Speaking about
the Cosmopolitan cover,

00:01:39.144 --> 00:01:43.450
do you think that those
covers send the right message

00:01:43.450 --> 00:01:46.310
or wrong message during
a time where obesity

00:01:46.310 --> 00:01:47.700
in the United States and in the world

00:01:47.700 --> 00:01:49.490
is becoming an epidemic of sorts?

00:01:49.490 --> 00:01:52.160
With COVID-19 we see worse
outcomes for patients

00:01:52.160 --> 00:01:54.140
who are diagnosed with obesity.

00:01:54.140 --> 00:01:56.631
Do you think those covers
are problematic, basically?

00:01:56.631 --> 00:01:58.599
- I think they're a little bit misleading.

00:01:58.599 --> 00:02:01.140
I think that the story itself,

00:02:01.140 --> 00:02:04.360
like the content that
they wrote is very nice

00:02:04.360 --> 00:02:06.356
and they talk about how
each of these women,

00:02:06.356 --> 00:02:09.960
what their relationship
with physical activity is

00:02:09.960 --> 00:02:12.206
and how they feel about
their health and their body,

00:02:12.206 --> 00:02:15.840
and it's not even about obesity at all.

00:02:15.840 --> 00:02:17.360
There were a number of people criticizing,

00:02:17.360 --> 00:02:20.650
saying that, well, especially when we know

00:02:20.650 --> 00:02:23.470
that people with obesity have
worse outcomes with COVID,

00:02:23.470 --> 00:02:25.760
this is irresponsible.

00:02:25.760 --> 00:02:28.630
And to me that feels a
little bit of a stretch,

00:02:28.630 --> 00:02:31.360
like I think they're just trying to say

00:02:31.360 --> 00:02:35.310
that people of all
different sizes can move,

00:02:35.310 --> 00:02:38.508
can be physically active,
can love their bodies.

00:02:38.508 --> 00:02:41.060
And I think that's a really good message.

00:02:41.060 --> 00:02:43.550
- I think they are ultimately
sending the message

00:02:43.550 --> 00:02:44.950
and I agree with you on that.

00:02:44.950 --> 00:02:46.830
I just think they're
doing it in a cheeky way,

00:02:46.830 --> 00:02:49.560
where they're trying to create
inflammatory conversation

00:02:49.560 --> 00:02:50.393
or maybe -
- Oh, for sure.

00:02:50.393 --> 00:02:51.450
- Productive

00:02:51.450 --> 00:02:53.095
inflammatory conversation.
- Yeah.

00:02:53.095 --> 00:02:54.040
No doubt.

00:02:54.040 --> 00:02:57.180
I mean, they could have said
this is loving your body,

00:02:57.180 --> 00:03:01.740
or you know, everybody is
worthy or what, you know,

00:03:01.740 --> 00:03:03.530
there's a number of different
things they could have put

00:03:03.530 --> 00:03:05.885
on those covers that would
have been pretty accurate

00:03:05.885 --> 00:03:07.570
and indisputable.

00:03:07.570 --> 00:03:10.125
- [Presenter] We frequently
see on the cover of magazines

00:03:10.125 --> 00:03:15.125
very slender bodies, thin
muscular, six packs, et cetera.

00:03:16.100 --> 00:03:20.440
It's led to true cases of
disordered eating going up

00:03:20.440 --> 00:03:21.690
and body dysmorphia.

00:03:21.690 --> 00:03:24.870
The sort of rebuttal to this is to say

00:03:24.870 --> 00:03:26.180
that's what's happening right now.

00:03:26.180 --> 00:03:29.150
Let's put people of
different sizes on magazines

00:03:29.150 --> 00:03:31.850
and let's take someone who
is diagnosed with obesity

00:03:31.850 --> 00:03:32.760
and put them on a cover.

00:03:32.760 --> 00:03:35.350
Is there a chance that that
can lead to disordered eating

00:03:35.350 --> 00:03:36.740
in the opposite direction

00:03:36.740 --> 00:03:40.040
of people feeling it's okay
for me to be this size,

00:03:40.040 --> 00:03:43.370
I should continue my
lifestyle, not make changes,

00:03:43.370 --> 00:03:46.100
not think about going
for bariatric surgery

00:03:46.100 --> 00:03:48.940
because the celebrities I
look up to are this way.

00:03:48.940 --> 00:03:50.370
- I think there's a balance there.

00:03:50.370 --> 00:03:53.395
I think that we can have the
covers, like the Cosmo ones

00:03:53.395 --> 00:03:56.810
and say, look, people
who are a bigger size

00:03:56.810 --> 00:04:01.200
can still be active and aspire
to having a healthy body

00:04:01.200 --> 00:04:03.990
versus saying, this is how we all are,

00:04:03.990 --> 00:04:05.750
and we should just live with it.

00:04:05.750 --> 00:04:07.941
You know, I think that's
a different message.

00:04:07.941 --> 00:04:11.190
In this country, about
three fourths of people

00:04:11.190 --> 00:04:12.812
have overweight or obesity.

00:04:12.812 --> 00:04:16.747
And if there were no negative
health consequences of that,

00:04:16.747 --> 00:04:18.526
maybe that would be fine.

00:04:18.526 --> 00:04:20.767
But the fact is, as we've talked about,

00:04:20.767 --> 00:04:24.520
all of that excess weight
brings with it risk

00:04:24.520 --> 00:04:27.230
for very serious problems that are costly

00:04:27.230 --> 00:04:30.435
to the individual that
inhibit their lifestyles

00:04:30.435 --> 00:04:32.835
and make it harder for them

00:04:32.835 --> 00:04:34.590
to live the life they might want.

00:04:34.590 --> 00:04:36.990
So I think we have to kind
of acknowledge both sides

00:04:36.990 --> 00:04:39.700
of it that no, we don't want
to be stigmatizing people

00:04:39.700 --> 00:04:42.240
who are bigger, but we
also want to recognize

00:04:42.240 --> 00:04:44.850
that obesity has a number
of medical problems

00:04:44.850 --> 00:04:45.980
associated with it.

00:04:45.980 --> 00:04:47.650
- The way that someone like you

00:04:47.650 --> 00:04:49.493
goes about treating individuals

00:04:49.493 --> 00:04:52.230
who are diagnosed with obesity

00:04:52.230 --> 00:04:53.930
or at least morbid obesity

00:04:53.930 --> 00:04:55.470
would be through a procedure known

00:04:55.470 --> 00:04:56.930
as bariatric surgery, right?

00:04:56.930 --> 00:04:58.120
That is your specialty.

00:04:58.120 --> 00:05:00.650
You obviously don't
think that is recommended

00:05:00.650 --> 00:05:04.310
for all patients, but it's
certainly one of the best

00:05:04.310 --> 00:05:05.570
treatments out there when it comes

00:05:05.570 --> 00:05:08.237
from an evidence perspective
of not only losing weight

00:05:08.237 --> 00:05:11.100
but keeping weight off long-term.

00:05:11.100 --> 00:05:14.220
And then also even getting
rid of some other diagnoses

00:05:14.220 --> 00:05:17.250
like diabetes, like high
cholesterol, et cetera.

00:05:17.250 --> 00:05:20.670
Why is it that bariatric
surgery has better

00:05:20.670 --> 00:05:24.060
long-term success in
keeping the weight off

00:05:24.060 --> 00:05:27.440
than let's say traditional diet
and exercise recommendations

00:05:27.440 --> 00:05:29.340
that your family medicine
doctor can give you.

00:05:29.340 --> 00:05:31.230
- [Arghavan] One thing we
know about losing weight

00:05:31.230 --> 00:05:34.590
with diet and exercise is
that only about 5% of people

00:05:34.590 --> 00:05:36.930
who lose weight that
way are able to maintain

00:05:36.930 --> 00:05:37.890
that weight loss.

00:05:37.890 --> 00:05:41.100
So all of us know people
who've had incredible success

00:05:41.100 --> 00:05:43.730
with diets and exercise,
and that's amazing

00:05:43.730 --> 00:05:45.340
and something to be really proud of

00:05:45.340 --> 00:05:46.860
because they've really beaten the odds.

00:05:46.860 --> 00:05:49.810
For the majority of people,
no matter how hard they try,

00:05:49.810 --> 00:05:52.631
it's very, very hard for them
to get to a healthier weight

00:05:52.631 --> 00:05:54.255
and size on their own.

00:05:54.255 --> 00:05:56.510
And that's where
bariatric surgery comes in

00:05:56.510 --> 00:05:57.880
and is really really helpful.

00:05:57.880 --> 00:05:59.380
We don't a hundred percent know

00:05:59.380 --> 00:06:01.750
in terms of the weight loss,
why it's more effective.

00:06:01.750 --> 00:06:05.560
We think that it helps reset
people's kind of set point

00:06:05.560 --> 00:06:06.393
if you will.

00:06:06.393 --> 00:06:08.073
A lot of us believe that everybody

00:06:08.073 --> 00:06:10.616
wants to be around a certain number

00:06:10.616 --> 00:06:13.880
in terms of weight or a
certain size in terms of BMI.

00:06:13.880 --> 00:06:15.700
We don't know how that develops

00:06:15.700 --> 00:06:17.320
but that seems to be what happens.

00:06:17.320 --> 00:06:18.600
One of the most magical things

00:06:18.600 --> 00:06:21.368
about bariatric surgery
is its effect on diabetes.

00:06:21.368 --> 00:06:24.210
Especially when we talk about specifically

00:06:24.210 --> 00:06:26.770
the gastric bypass, we
have many, many patients

00:06:26.770 --> 00:06:29.460
who have diabetes, are on insulin,

00:06:29.460 --> 00:06:31.020
maybe other medications for it

00:06:31.020 --> 00:06:32.840
and then they have surgery today.

00:06:32.840 --> 00:06:35.120
And then today their blood sugar is normal

00:06:35.120 --> 00:06:36.870
and they no longer need those medications,

00:06:36.870 --> 00:06:38.680
so that's really magic.

00:06:38.680 --> 00:06:40.740
- Absolutely, I mean anytime you can cure

00:06:40.740 --> 00:06:43.010
what we consider to be
an incurable illness,

00:06:43.010 --> 00:06:44.360
it's absolutely amazing.

00:06:44.360 --> 00:06:47.440
Talk about the challenges
that your patients face,

00:06:47.440 --> 00:06:49.360
and actually my patients as well,

00:06:49.360 --> 00:06:53.140
in trying to get bariatric surgery covered

00:06:53.140 --> 00:06:54.740
from an insurance perspective,

00:06:54.740 --> 00:06:56.650
'cause right now, I believe in the past,

00:06:56.650 --> 00:07:00.070
you've mentioned that only
1% of eligible patients

00:07:00.070 --> 00:07:02.700
actually do end up
getting bariatric surgery.

00:07:02.700 --> 00:07:04.640
Why is that number so low?

00:07:04.640 --> 00:07:06.413
Can we impact it in a meaningful way?

00:07:06.413 --> 00:07:09.000
- I think the obstacles are multiple.

00:07:09.000 --> 00:07:11.370
One is people in the past have thought

00:07:11.370 --> 00:07:13.280
that bariatric surgery is dangerous.

00:07:13.280 --> 00:07:15.547
It turns out that's not true anymore.

00:07:15.547 --> 00:07:20.100
The risk to dying of dying
within 30 days of surgery

00:07:20.100 --> 00:07:22.630
is less than it is for having
your gallbladder taken out

00:07:22.630 --> 00:07:24.780
which people do every day in this country.

00:07:24.780 --> 00:07:28.200
Another is the perception
that it's the easy way out,

00:07:28.200 --> 00:07:30.099
that if you have bariatric surgery,

00:07:30.099 --> 00:07:32.420
then you have no willpower,

00:07:32.420 --> 00:07:35.230
you can't do it on your own, et cetera.

00:07:35.230 --> 00:07:37.430
And really that's not it at all.

00:07:37.430 --> 00:07:39.440
I think the people who
come to us for surgery

00:07:39.440 --> 00:07:40.750
are some of the strongest people

00:07:40.750 --> 00:07:42.840
because what they're
acknowledging and realizing

00:07:42.840 --> 00:07:45.360
is that they have tried
all these other things

00:07:45.360 --> 00:07:47.970
that don't involve surgery
and nothing has worked.

00:07:47.970 --> 00:07:50.020
And rather than just
giving up, they're saying

00:07:50.020 --> 00:07:51.120
what else is out there?

00:07:51.120 --> 00:07:51.953
What else can I do?

00:07:51.953 --> 00:07:53.590
Because I want to take
control of my health

00:07:53.590 --> 00:07:55.350
and I want to have a healthier life.

00:07:55.350 --> 00:07:57.270
Another issue is time.

00:07:57.270 --> 00:07:59.120
I believe you would know
more about this than I do

00:07:59.120 --> 00:08:00.620
but in your office, you're the one

00:08:00.620 --> 00:08:01.540
who's seeing these patients

00:08:01.540 --> 00:08:03.700
before they come to see someone like me.

00:08:03.700 --> 00:08:06.470
And you're often managing,
you know, diabetes,

00:08:06.470 --> 00:08:08.550
high blood pressure, high cholesterol,

00:08:08.550 --> 00:08:11.860
maybe a mood disorder, you
know, all these other things.

00:08:11.860 --> 00:08:14.700
I think sometimes it can be
hard to talk about obesity.

00:08:14.700 --> 00:08:17.290
And also, you know, I
think you've mentioned this

00:08:17.290 --> 00:08:18.590
in some of your previous videos,

00:08:18.590 --> 00:08:20.420
there's a lot of
sensitivity around obesity

00:08:20.420 --> 00:08:22.110
and how do we talk to people about it

00:08:22.110 --> 00:08:26.410
in a way that is engaging
and brings them to the table

00:08:26.410 --> 00:08:28.170
and makes everybody feel like we care,

00:08:28.170 --> 00:08:30.040
that we as physicians
care about their health

00:08:30.040 --> 00:08:32.460
as opposed to judging
or blaming or shaming.

00:08:32.460 --> 00:08:34.970
And then the last like maybe biggest issue

00:08:34.970 --> 00:08:36.137
is insurance coverage.

00:08:36.137 --> 00:08:40.152
I believe, as many of us
do, that part of the reason

00:08:40.152 --> 00:08:42.580
most insurance plans
or many insurance plans

00:08:42.580 --> 00:08:43.900
don't cover bariatric surgery

00:08:43.900 --> 00:08:47.430
is because of weight
stigma and obesity stigma.

00:08:47.430 --> 00:08:50.440
Basically people think
it's not a health issue

00:08:50.440 --> 00:08:52.540
but can you imagine if
there were a plan that said,

00:08:52.540 --> 00:08:55.940
no, you can't have a
coronary artery bypass graft.

00:08:55.940 --> 00:08:57.590
You have heart disease, but you're stuck.

00:08:57.590 --> 00:08:59.830
Good luck with it, it's a
lifestyle you've chosen.

00:08:59.830 --> 00:09:02.590
- Not only is it beneficial
to the patient, very clearly.

00:09:02.590 --> 00:09:05.257
It also should be beneficial
to the entire system

00:09:05.257 --> 00:09:07.710
because when a patient loses, you know,

00:09:07.710 --> 00:09:09.320
a significant portion of their weight,

00:09:09.320 --> 00:09:11.730
if they're a patient
diagnosed with morbid obesity

00:09:11.730 --> 00:09:14.070
they will have better outcomes,

00:09:14.070 --> 00:09:16.540
will use the emergency room less,

00:09:16.540 --> 00:09:20.370
will be less likely to need
a stent, a cardiac cath,

00:09:20.370 --> 00:09:22.700
will take less medications longterm.

00:09:22.700 --> 00:09:25.570
So wouldn't that be even
a cost-effective measure

00:09:25.570 --> 00:09:26.932
for them to implement?

00:09:26.932 --> 00:09:30.810
And I don't know why it
is that a lot of insurers

00:09:30.810 --> 00:09:32.020
don't look at it in that way.

00:09:32.020 --> 00:09:34.390
- Yeah, I mean, I don't
totally know either,

00:09:34.390 --> 00:09:35.420
I haven't been in their shoes,

00:09:35.420 --> 00:09:38.040
but what I think is
happening is an artifact

00:09:38.040 --> 00:09:40.099
of our healthcare system in this country.

00:09:40.099 --> 00:09:43.550
Basically in our country,
people change their insurance

00:09:43.550 --> 00:09:45.010
every time they change a job, right?

00:09:45.010 --> 00:09:47.330
And sometimes even when they
have the same job, you know,

00:09:47.330 --> 00:09:49.680
there's a new election
period for their benefits

00:09:49.680 --> 00:09:51.040
and they switch to some other company.

00:09:51.040 --> 00:09:53.280
In order for one company
to see the benefit,

00:09:53.280 --> 00:09:55.360
that long-term benefit in cost savings

00:09:55.360 --> 00:09:56.390
after bariatric surgery,

00:09:56.390 --> 00:09:57.980
that means that patient
would have to stay with them

00:09:57.980 --> 00:09:59.040
for years and years

00:09:59.040 --> 00:10:00.770
and many patients do not

00:10:00.770 --> 00:10:02.060
because either they change their jobs

00:10:02.060 --> 00:10:03.980
or there's something else
that they need on their plan

00:10:03.980 --> 00:10:04.813
and they switch.

00:10:04.813 --> 00:10:06.800
The only way I think we would
really reap those benefits

00:10:06.800 --> 00:10:10.600
is if every plan from every
insurance company covered it

00:10:10.600 --> 00:10:12.750
and then they would all be
seeing those cost savings

00:10:12.750 --> 00:10:15.350
as patients move from
one system to another.

00:10:15.350 --> 00:10:17.798
- You know, it's unfortunate
that we've come to the point

00:10:17.798 --> 00:10:21.161
where we have to rely on a surgery

00:10:21.161 --> 00:10:23.595
because we don't have a clear cut way

00:10:23.595 --> 00:10:27.580
for 95% of people to
be able to lose weight

00:10:27.580 --> 00:10:28.754
on their own and keep it off long-term.

00:10:28.754 --> 00:10:31.440
Why do you think that number, 5%,

00:10:31.440 --> 00:10:34.200
of being able to keep weight
off long-term is so low?

00:10:34.200 --> 00:10:37.390
Is it something to do
with adherence to diets?

00:10:37.390 --> 00:10:41.300
Is it the lack of good
information on nutrition?

00:10:41.300 --> 00:10:44.750
The fact that there's a level
of willpower that's involved

00:10:44.750 --> 00:10:47.570
in this, where do you
think that 5% stems from?

00:10:47.570 --> 00:10:49.550
- I mean, just like anything
else it's multifactorial,

00:10:49.550 --> 00:10:51.480
there a number of
different factors involved.

00:10:51.480 --> 00:10:54.040
Like one, for example, is
we know a lot of people

00:10:54.040 --> 00:10:57.410
with obesity live in
parts of towns and cities

00:10:57.410 --> 00:10:59.860
where they don't have
access to healthy food

00:10:59.860 --> 00:11:01.717
or not easy access to healthy food.

00:11:01.717 --> 00:11:03.770
We also know that healthy food

00:11:03.770 --> 00:11:05.900
in a lot of cases is more expensive.

00:11:05.900 --> 00:11:09.010
So that means people who
are making less money

00:11:09.010 --> 00:11:11.630
or lower socioeconomic class level

00:11:11.630 --> 00:11:13.320
don't have access to healthier foods

00:11:13.320 --> 00:11:17.160
and so even if they can get
by for some period of time

00:11:17.160 --> 00:11:18.710
and make modifications, you know,

00:11:18.710 --> 00:11:21.320
there's real budgetary
constraints that come into play.

00:11:21.320 --> 00:11:22.540
- How do you talk to patients

00:11:22.540 --> 00:11:25.250
that may not be candidates
for bariatric surgery

00:11:25.250 --> 00:11:27.071
because you're certified in
obesity medicine as well?

00:11:27.071 --> 00:11:29.720
How do you counsel those
patients saying like, look,

00:11:29.720 --> 00:11:31.790
maybe bariatric surgery isn't for you.

00:11:31.790 --> 00:11:34.770
There's a 95% chance you're
going to fail in losing weight

00:11:34.770 --> 00:11:36.360
but here's how you can be successful

00:11:36.360 --> 00:11:37.850
'cause it's a daunting task.

00:11:37.850 --> 00:11:38.960
- I would tell anybody,

00:11:38.960 --> 00:11:40.440
whether they're having
bariatric surgery or not,

00:11:40.440 --> 00:11:41.620
more or less the same thing

00:11:41.620 --> 00:11:44.670
which is that there has
to be a lifestyle change.

00:11:44.670 --> 00:11:47.948
As long as we are thinking
about weight loss as a diet,

00:11:47.948 --> 00:11:49.830
as a temporary thing,

00:11:49.830 --> 00:11:52.470
especially in a way that we're
going to deprive ourselves,

00:11:52.470 --> 00:11:54.970
that is not sustainable for anybody.

00:11:54.970 --> 00:11:57.380
The only time that you
will really be successful,

00:11:57.380 --> 00:11:59.455
and this is true for those
who've had surgery as well,

00:11:59.455 --> 00:12:02.570
is committing to living
life a different way,

00:12:02.570 --> 00:12:06.400
committing to living and
eating and moving differently

00:12:06.400 --> 00:12:07.570
than you did before.

00:12:07.570 --> 00:12:10.231
- There was a quote that I
wrote down in my book here

00:12:10.231 --> 00:12:13.440
from your Medscape
article, someone commented,

00:12:13.440 --> 00:12:15.398
I believe it was a nurse, an RN.

00:12:15.398 --> 00:12:19.630
This person wrote body
positivity is not about health.

00:12:19.630 --> 00:12:21.900
It's about combating discrimination

00:12:21.900 --> 00:12:23.980
against marginalized bodies.

00:12:23.980 --> 00:12:25.860
And I think that's such a powerful quote.

00:12:25.860 --> 00:12:29.260
The body positivity movement
should be about fighting

00:12:29.260 --> 00:12:30.239
this discrimination,

00:12:30.239 --> 00:12:34.860
not about saying that diagnosis
of obesity is a false one

00:12:34.860 --> 00:12:37.400
or made up one, or it's a judgmental one.

00:12:37.400 --> 00:12:40.439
It's an objective
measure of someone's risk

00:12:40.439 --> 00:12:42.251
and current health.

00:12:42.251 --> 00:12:44.480
Now it doesn't mean that
you're completely unhealthy

00:12:44.480 --> 00:12:46.440
if you fall into one of
these BMI categories.

00:12:46.440 --> 00:12:48.630
No, 'cause again, spectrum.

00:12:48.630 --> 00:12:51.020
But the fact that if someone
discriminates against you

00:12:51.020 --> 00:12:53.182
for being in this group,

00:12:53.182 --> 00:12:56.386
that's what body positivity
should really be focused on.

00:12:56.386 --> 00:12:57.760
- I think it's like a yes and.

00:12:57.760 --> 00:12:59.960
Yes, you should feel good about your body.

00:12:59.960 --> 00:13:02.030
Everybody deserves to feel
good about their body.

00:13:02.030 --> 00:13:03.970
Everybody is worthy of love.

00:13:03.970 --> 00:13:07.641
And there are negative health
consequences for obesity.

00:13:07.641 --> 00:13:10.830
Both these things are true
and they are not in opposition

00:13:10.830 --> 00:13:12.240
and anyone who tells you otherwise

00:13:12.240 --> 00:13:13.710
I think is stirring up trouble.

00:13:13.710 --> 00:13:15.210
- I would like for you
to have the last word,

00:13:15.210 --> 00:13:16.146
you're the expert.

00:13:16.146 --> 00:13:18.730
Tell us what message you'd like
to leave the audience with.

00:13:18.730 --> 00:13:20.980
- I just would love
everybody to understand

00:13:20.980 --> 00:13:23.830
that when your doctor talks
to you about your weight

00:13:23.830 --> 00:13:26.856
it's most times not because
they're trying to attack you

00:13:26.856 --> 00:13:28.550
but because they want to help you.

00:13:28.550 --> 00:13:29.900
If you're not ready to talk about it,

00:13:29.900 --> 00:13:31.580
you can just say so,

00:13:31.580 --> 00:13:33.100
but keep in mind that
you can always come back

00:13:33.100 --> 00:13:34.220
to that conversation.

00:13:34.220 --> 00:13:37.110
And in the end, we're all in it together

00:13:37.110 --> 00:13:40.246
to help us live happy and healthy lives.

00:13:40.246 --> 00:13:43.163
(upbeat pop music)

